CORE dfcc-only
main.c
--dfcc main --apply-loop-contracts _ --no-standard-checks
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* x is 0 or 1: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^\[.*\.assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^\[.*\.loop_assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^Invariant check failed$
--
DFCC instruments c_set before l_loop, and a_mid after it. To infer the
assigns clause of the loop of l_loop, inlining reaches the body of a_mid,
which is not instrumented yet and so passes no write set to c_set, which is
instrumented and has the write set as its first parameter. Then that
parameter got &x and c_set's parameter p got 1, which stopped the inliner.
The loop havocs x, so the assertion after it fails.
